Analyze the following {language} code for Domain-Driven Design (DDD) violations.

For each violation found, report:
- pattern: one of: anemic-domain-model, business-logic-in-service, ubiquitous-language-violation, aggregate-boundary-violation, missing-value-object
- class_or_method: name of the offending class or method
- line: approximate line number
- severity: high, medium, or low
- violation: what DDD principle is broken
- suggestion: how to fix using DDD patterns

Patterns to detect:
- anemic-domain-model: entity/aggregate with only getters/setters and no meaningful behavior methods — all logic in services
- business-logic-in-service: service/controller/handler containing domain calculations, validations, or business rules that belong in the domain model
- ubiquitous-language-violation: technical names (UserData, ProcessItem, HandleRequest) for concepts that should use domain language (Order, Payment, Shipment)
- aggregate-boundary-violation: directly accessing or modifying internal entities of another aggregate (reaching inside another aggregate root)
- missing-value-object: primitive obsession — using raw strings/ints/floats where a Value Object would encapsulate invariants (Money, Email, Address, PhoneNumber as primitives)

